---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
Query the graph at multiple resolutions

Usage: specforge query [OPTIONS] <ENTITY>

Arguments:
  <ENTITY>
          Entity ID to query

Options:
      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --depth <DEPTH>
          Number of hops from the entity (0 = entity only)
          
          [default: 1]

      --kinds <KINDS>
          Keep only entities of these kinds (comma-separated, or the flag repeated); the queried entity always stays

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output detail level

          Possible values:
          - graph:   every entity with its fields, file and line (Graph Protocol)
          - context: every entity's fields, annotations, methods and verify, its headline on top, without file and line, for an agent
          - brief:   id, kind and title
          
          [default: graph]

      --include-coverage
          Give every entity its coverage status

      --inputs <RULE>
          Instead of the neighbourhood, list what this judged rule reads for the entity: each input with its digest, the verdict's state, and the basis `set_verdict` checks

      --cite <ENTITY>
          With --inputs: an entity the verdict will cite (the flag repeated)

      --cite-file <FILE>
          With --inputs: a file the verdict will cite, from the project root

      --cite-test <TEST>
          With --inputs: a test the verdict will cite, by name

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the graph format's JSON answer, one per line, e.g. '.nodes[kind=plan].id'. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[kind=<k>]` (the items whose `kind` is <k>). One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. It takes --format graph or json, and neither --inputs nor --include-coverage.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
